I was going to write a dissertation on Digital hardware, software and
firmware, but decided against doing so.

I worked for Digital from 1986 till 2001 (Compaq) in Manufacturing
Engineering.

We did have our own firmware as our software OpenVMS, ULTRIX, OSF/1 and
later Tru64 UNIX took advantage of the correctly implimented features in the
various SCSI devices we sold with our systems. When our drives had trouble
reading data, they would log defects in the error log so that a system
manager could figure out that he had hardware that was going bad and make a
plan to replace it. On Windows, it just tries again and again and again
until it gets the data and nobody is ever aware that it was on the 120th
retry that the data finally came through. It was this and many other things
that leads me to the point that OpenVMS is probably the most highest
availability Operating System ever invented. Smart customers still demand
OpenVMS today.

rtt




