At DIGITAL, we were not perfect. There are a number of customers or former
customers worldwide who are or were pissed off at us. That said, the
operative line within DIGITAL was always "Do the right thing", most people
followed that, some didn't, and altough most of the time that was meant to
define how we engaged our customers, some interpreted it to mean how we
engaged our business. We made mistakes, all kinds and we did good things
too.

One mistake we made that I was involved in on the peripheray, was a value
engineered Alpha for the Desktop called Mustang. We had employed an external
firm to spin the ASIC's for this machine. When it came time to do the final
spin, third version as I remember, the cheif designer at the external firm
had gone to work for someone else and the Mustang and all the hard work we
put inot it got scrapped.

Another mistake we made was setting up a relationship with Ollevitti to
re-brand and re-market their beautiful looking Notebooks and Laptops. The
product was hopeless in it's mechanical design and flexed so much that
numerous units shorted out the circuit board and would spontaneously reboot
and crash during use. We ended up recalling al of those units, possibly 10's
of thousands of units by the time the decisin came down. We had a contract
though with them to sell their product, so we ended up building their
desktops and re-selling them. The problem was, whenever we were building the
product, a lot of our workforce would cal in sick. It turns out that in
Italy, the amount of time spent maintaining metal stamping machinery is
minimal so a lot of metal gets stamped and ripped, leaving sharp edges on
the metal. The poeple bulding this product always ended up with sharp cuts
on their hands, so they started avaoiding the build of the product by caling
in sick.

The AlphaServer 2100, first of the Alpha Servers, the one wiht the 8 EISA
slots and 3 PCI slots, was partially designed by young engineers fresh from
graduation, they were given thedesign of the Multi-IO module, the one with
the SCSI, NIC and BUS interface on it. I watched the revision level of the
prototype modules rapidly rise from A to B and all the way to G before we
finally had something we could goto market with. At about the same time that
we were going to introduce htis product, Apple showed up with what could be
considered an identical product asking us to quote on the build costs, in
case they wanted us to build it form them. I thought at the time that it was
a great opportunity to sell them our product instead and sent an email to
Bob Palmer. His office ocntacted me within hours to make sure that we were
talking about the same firm and then later after conversing with Steve Jobs,
I was called back and thanked for my interest and that unfortunately Apple
had declined the offer. this just goes to show that even the little people
could make an influence within Digital by contributing their ideas and
thoughts.

On another note, we were getting beaten in Manufacturing aout the cost of
building product, but when you consider a PC with 35 screws to assemble and
four different sizes, labour is a premium. So I sent another email to Bob
Palmer identifying a Apple presentation where Steve Jobs assembled a Quadra
series machine in less than 5 minutes from scratch before the audience. This
comapred to our experienced manufacturing force taking over 35 minutes to
build a similar product. Within an hour, Bob's secratary was calling me
asking if I could identify the Apple conference, and fortunately I got the
year right, and they were able to get a Quicktime video of the even to
watch. A few hours later the Engineering group responsible for cabinet
design was on the phone, and telling me about all the news cabinets they
were on the edge of introducing. We neve did get down to 5 minutes to build
a box, but we came close to 10 minutes.

There were numerous times where we got it right. We just never managed to
sell it. The Alpha Chip comes to mind. so too the VAX design. Probably the
VAX was the best design effort ever, in the world. Becasue the design of the
VAX was based on a DEC Standard, it followed a rigorous process, both in
terms of hardware and software, whch most probbaly contributes to it's solid
and stable evolution. OpenVMS today, at version 7, still runs on my MicroVAX
3100 Model 10, a machine that was introduced around OpenVMS Version 4. Not
many OS's can claim that. Heck, the little machine will een play properly in
a mixed clsuter today. The Fault Tolerant series of VAXen were probably the
hardest things to sell. They were non-stop machines that did the best
fault-tolerant, without requireing one to write their software to some funny
software librabry, just plug in and run. Unfortunately, we were just dipping
our toes in the water with what we introduced, and never did get up to full
steam on the product set. I used to love kicking the plugs out of the wall
or pulling memory cards or disks and watching them continue to run.

rtt




