"Richard Tomkins" <tomkinsr@istop.com> writes:

>One mistake we made that I was involved in on the peripheray, was a value
>engineered Alpha for the Desktop called Mustang. We had employed an external
>firm to spin the ASIC's for this machine. When it came time to do the final
>spin, third version as I remember, the cheif designer at the external firm
>had gone to work for someone else and the Mustang and all the hard work we
>put inot it got scrapped.

The Mustang was scrapped because it used the LCA (21066) chip.  It was
decided they were too low performance. It was lower performance than the 
original (21064) Alpha, although I don't remember the details of how.
It wasn't a complete write-off, the same basic idea was redone as Mustang 
II, or the Alphastation 200 4/xxx, which were quite popular (I'm typing 
this on one now in fact).  These used regular EV4 alphas.

I worked for a qualification group qualifying the prototype Mustang IIs.
My supervisor even went around the Mill after hours gathering up Mustang I
boxes from scrap heaps.  When a new revison of the CPU boards came out
(or when 233 MHz boards became available, 166 MHz were the first) instead
of scrapping the old boards we'd build extra systems from the old Mustang I
boxes and used them in tests where the revision was not important.
(CPU and riser board needed to be replaced, box, power supply and
periphials could be reused)  He also had some connection in the prototype
manufacturing group and got extra CPU and riser cards, so between the
two methods we had more systems than we should have, so we requested
fewer and saved Digital some money.


-- 
-Mike


